/**
\page peano_utilities Utilities

## d-dimensional loops

Peano builds every core library for two and three spatial dimensions. The preprocessor macro
`DIMENSIONS`, which the build system sets per target from the CMake `DIMENSION` keyword, selects
the dimension at compile time, and the cache variable `SUPPORTED_DIMENSIONS` lists the dimensions
of a build.

The header `peano4/Loop.h` offers the d-dimensional loops:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
peano4::dForEach(20, [&](const tarch::la::Vector<DIMENSIONS, int>& k) {
  // k holds the d-dimensional index of the current entry of the 20^d patch.
  const tarch::la::Vector<DIMENSIONS, double> somePosition =
    offset + tarch::la::convertScalar<double>(k) * h;
  [...]
});
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

peano4::dForEach also takes a vector of per-axis ranges, and peano4::dForEachCorner visits the
2^d corners of a cube together with their linearised index. Both are constexpr functions, so they
take the execution space of their caller: Host code passes host lambdas, and a kernel passes
device lambdas.

peano4::dLinearised() converts a d-dimensional index into one integer, and peano4::dDelinearised()
converts the integer back. peano4::dLinearisedExclusive() and peano4::dDelinearisedExclusive() do
the same for the indices of a face, which skip one dimension.
*/
